These docs are read along three tracks, each of which answers a different question and can be followed alone, beside a reference to look things up in and an archive of how the library came to be.

## Three tracks

Two tracks say what the library is today, and are rewritten whenever it changes.
The third says what it rests on.

- **The [guide](../../guide/) is top-down.**
  It starts from what a user writes (a model, a distribution, a density to evaluate) and reaches into the machinery only as far as an example needs.
  Read it to use skoro.
- **The [concepts](../../concepts/) are bottom-up.**
  They build the library from its primitive objects upward, each idea resting only on the ones before it.
  Read them to understand why skoro works the way it does, or before changing it.
- **The [mathematics](../../math/) is the ground under both.**
  It is bottom-up too, and independent of skoro: every piece of mathematics the pages rely on that would be true if the library did not exist, one idea to a note, such as a retraction, the normal distribution on a curved space or the Laplace approximation.
  Read it to know why what the library computes is right.

The tracks meet.
A guide page links down into the concepts for the curious, and a concepts page links the notes it needs, so that a reader may start on any track and cross to another where a question takes them.

## The mathematics in order

The notes stand in an order, in three parts: spaces and their charts, probability on those spaces, and least squares with the Gaussian approximations built on it.
Each note rests only on the notes before it, so that read from the first to the last they tell the whole of the mathematics once.
A note says which earlier notes it builds on, so a reader who arrives in the middle knows how far back to go.
The map on that page draws the whole network, together with the archive's documents that use it.

What is skoro's own, a choice among true things or a statement about something the library defines, is not in the notes.
It is in the concepts, which say what it is, and in the archive, which argued for it.

## Looking things up

The [reference](../../reference/) holds what is looked up rather than read: the crates, each with what it is for and the order in which to read its modules, and how skoro is developed.

## How it came to be

The [archive](../../archive/) keeps the record of every piece of development.
A feature's entry holds the mathematics worked out for it, the implementation companion that turned the mathematics into a design, and the plan it was built by, each kept as it was approved.
A decision's entry holds the argument for it.

The archive is history, and history is not rewritten.
One thing in a finished entry may change: where a note it refers to has since been rewritten, the reference is brought up to date, and the entry says which later entry did so.
Where the concepts track _tells_ you what is, an entry _discusses_ what might be, as it stood when it was written.
When the library later changes, a new entry revises the old one, and the old one says so at its top.
Every page lists the entries that made it what it is, under "How this came to be", and every entry lists the pages it changed.

> [!NOTE]
> Every document is also served as Markdown (follow "View as Markdown" at its foot), and [llms.txt](../../llms.txt) indexes all of them for language models.

## Where to go next

Pick the track that matches what you came for.
Each card opens the track's first page, and every page ends by pointing to the next.

